for more on the fallout from the firing  of James Comey we're joined by CBS News  justice reporter Paula Reid she's in our  Washington bureau Paula good morning  good morning  Paula Democrats are raising the specter  of possible intimidation and obstruction  of justice what exactly are the legal  ramifications here Anthony at this point  we don't have enough evidence in the  public record to support obstruction of  justice you would need to prove that the  president knew what he was doing was  going to impact the investigation  sources are telling us that in a lot of  ways what's happened over the past few  days has invigorated FBI investigators  it's important to note there is another  crime and that is lying to the FBI that  is a crime so anyone who's interviewed  in the course of this investigation they  need to make sure that they have their  facts straight before they talk to  investigators otherwise that is a chart  that could stick Paula we're starting to  hear names to replace direct former  director Comey what can you tell us  about them and what will be expected of  them well earlier in the week the  Attorney General and his deputy rod  Rosen Stein they interviewed four  candidates all top level counter  intelligence officials currently serving  in government and then today they're  going to interview a more diverse pool  of candidates among them as Senator John  Cornyn and also two lesser known names a  lawyer named Alice Fisher and also a  judge Michael Garcia out of New York now  both of them have previously held top  Department of Justice jobs one other  name is also the current acting director  of the FBI Andrew McCabe the president  is not trying to find someone who is  qualified but will also not look like  he's trying to replace Comey  with one of his cronies so they're  looking at all these candidates and more  but these are the eight that we know  have or will be interviewed later today  all a read in our Washington bureau  thank you Paul
 

